1
00:00:08,265 --> 00:00:14,507
This is everything Halstead has on today.
One board, every job, sorted by the state

2
00:00:14,507 --> 00:00:20,824
it is in — so the column a job sits in
tells you where it has got to without opening

3
00:00:20,824 --> 00:00:21,500
anything.

4
00:00:21,967 --> 00:00:27,760
The same work, read three ways. The
board for what is moving, the table when you

5
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:33,769
want to sort and compare a hundred rows,
and the schedule when the question is what

6
00:00:33,769 --> 00:00:35,435
day something lands on.

7
00:00:35,886 --> 00:00:41,403
And when a client rings about one specific
thing, you search for it. Title, client,

8
00:00:41,403 --> 00:00:46,056
whoever is on it — you rarely need
to remember more than a word of it.

9
00:00:46,508 --> 00:00:51,866
A new job starts with the thing that
actually needs doing. Write it the way you

10
00:00:51,866 --> 00:00:57,359
would say it on the phone, and set how
much of a hurry it is in — the rest of the

11
00:00:57,359 --> 00:00:59,325
form waits until you need it.

12
00:00:59,779 --> 00:01:05,525
Choosing the client brings their address
across with it. Pin that address on the map

13
00:01:05,525 --> 00:01:11,202
once, and every job there afterwards knows
where it is — the route, the arrival and

14
00:01:11,202 --> 00:01:13,665
the time on site all follow from it.

15
00:01:14,120 --> 00:01:19,922
Then the person. They get it on their
phone the moment you save, with the address

16
00:01:19,922 --> 00:01:25,080
already on it — nobody is ringing
the depot to ask where they are going.

17
00:01:25,533 --> 00:01:31,462
The job now has a page of its own, and
this is where it lives for the rest of its

18
00:01:31,462 --> 00:01:37,464
life. Everything about it is on one
screen: what it is, who it belongs to, and how

19
00:01:37,464 --> 00:01:39,001
far along it has got.

20
00:01:39,452 --> 00:01:45,179
If the work has steps, they go on the job
itself. A checklist written at the desk is

21
00:01:45,179 --> 00:01:50,701
the difference between an engineer who
knows what finished looks like and one who

22
00:01:50,701 --> 00:01:55,474
has to ring in and ask — and it is
ticked off from the phone, on site.

23
00:01:55,926 --> 00:02:01,664
Underneath there is room for the parts of
a job that only turn up once somebody is

24
00:02:01,664 --> 00:02:07,192
standing in front of it: smaller jobs
inside this one, photographs of what they

25
00:02:07,192 --> 00:02:11,390
found, and the documents that
have to go back to the client.

26
00:02:11,841 --> 00:02:17,891
A job moves through your steps, not ours.
These buttons come from the workflow your

27
00:02:17,891 --> 00:02:23,869
organisation set up, so what comes next
is whatever your business says comes next.

28
00:02:24,320 --> 00:02:30,532
And when there is something to say about
a job, it is said on the job. Not in a

29
00:02:30,532 --> 00:02:36,901
message that scrolls away by Thursday —
here, against the work it is about, where

30
00:02:36,901 --> 00:02:39,181
the next person will find it.

31
00:02:39,633 --> 00:02:47,015
Everything that happened to it is kept:
who raised it, who was put on it, every step

32
00:02:47,015 --> 00:02:54,221
it moved through, and what was said. Six
months from now, that trail is the answer

33
00:02:54,221 --> 00:02:56,769
to what happened on this job.

